145
00:11:39,241 --> 00:11:43,740
At number six are the products of the digital revolution.

146
00:11:46,300 --> 00:11:50,180
Can you remember a world where your TV only had a handful of channels?

147
00:11:51,400 --> 00:11:54,700
And if you missed your favorite program, tough luck?

148
00:11:56,420 --> 00:11:58,420
Digital technology changed all that.

149
00:11:59,820 --> 00:12:05,480
Step into any electronics store and you'll be surrounded by widescreen digital TVs,

150
00:12:05,481 --> 00:12:12,480
DVDs, cameras, and video games.

151
00:12:12,481 --> 00:12:20,480
These digital devices have changed the way we entertain, play, and document.

152
00:12:20,481 --> 00:12:24,480
A digital system uses intermittent electric pulse values,

153
00:12:24,481 --> 00:12:32,480
symbolized by the binary numbers 1 or 0, to represent information.

154
00:12:32,481 --> 00:12:34,000
Take a CD, for example.

155
00:12:34,780 --> 00:12:37,840
More information can be crammed into smaller and smaller spaces,

156
00:12:38,360 --> 00:12:40,900
and the quality remains constant with each reproduction.

157
00:12:43,900 --> 00:12:47,420
The CD has got a bunch of pits that are burned into it,

158
00:12:47,620 --> 00:12:50,920
and you have a laser that looks at those pits.

159
00:12:50,921 --> 00:12:52,760
So if there's a pit, that's a one.

160
00:12:52,900 --> 00:12:54,240
If there's no pit, that's a zero.

161
00:12:54,580 --> 00:12:58,220
So your average CD holds about 50 million bits of information.

162
00:12:58,680 --> 00:13:00,340
So 50 million ones and zeros.

163
00:13:00,341 --> 00:13:07,340
Those ones and zeros form a spiral pattern that can become notes of music.

164
00:13:07,341 --> 00:13:14,340
The CD premiered in 1982 and soon replaced vinyl records.

165
00:13:14,341 --> 00:13:21,340
In 2008 alone, U.S. consumers bought 361 million music CDs.

166
00:13:21,341 --> 00:13:32,920
DVDs came out in 1993 and by 2008 Americans spent more than 23 billion dollars a year in sales and

167
00:13:32,921 --> 00:13:44,780
rentals a DVD is very similar to a CD but has a larger data capacity that's because the pits are

168
00:13:44,781 --> 00:13:51,780
four times smaller on a DVD, making the spiral tighter and longer.

169
00:13:52,120 --> 00:13:57,020
DVDs increase their capacity to almost 26 times that of CDs, because they can have up

170
00:13:57,021 --> 00:14:02,940
to four layers of data.

171
00:14:02,941 --> 00:14:06,960
But it's still not enough digital information for a feature film.

172
00:14:06,961 --> 00:14:15,560
For that, a second technology is needed, called Video MPEG-2 Compression.

173
00:14:15,561 --> 00:14:24,960
MPEG-2 Compression reduces the size of a video file by reusing any identical video information.

174
00:14:24,961 --> 00:14:29,560
Rather than storing data from each frame, Digital Compression lets one frame borrow

175
00:14:29,561 --> 00:14:35,040
from the previous frame any information that didn't change.

176
00:14:35,041 --> 00:14:39,580
So each new frame contains only the data that relates to how the picture is changed from

177
00:14:39,581 --> 00:14:44,160
the previous frame.

178
00:14:44,161 --> 00:14:49,580
Digital photography also uses those ones and zeros, which are translated into pixels or

179
00:14:49,581 --> 00:14:52,540
dots of light.

180
00:14:52,541 --> 00:14:56,440
No waiting to see how the film developed.

181
00:14:56,441 --> 00:15:00,360
